      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- **Proposed, pending changes:** The FY2026 and FY2027 President's budgets propose reorganizing BLS, Census, and BEA under Commerce's Under Secretary for Economic Affairs \u2014 not yet enacted, carried forward in FY2027 (DOL CBJs). Both budgets state BLS \"will refocus efforts\" on PFEIs, statutory programs, and programs in use in current law, an explicit signal that non-qualifying programs like ATUS are candidates for suspension.\n- **Statements by officials/experts:** Former Commissioner Groshen and the ASA community repeatedly name ATUS among the most likely programs to be scaled back or cancelled (Marketplace; LinkedIn/ASA posts). The FY2027 CBJ does show BLS proposing to suspend the new NLSY cohort to protect other programs \u2014 demonstrating the administration's willingness to suspend non-PFEI survey programs.\n- **Administration actions affecting the data environment:** disbanding of BLS/BEA/Census advisory committees including FESAC, suspension of the FSRDC Restricted Data Access program (which affects researcher access to ATUS restricted files), and broad data-disappearance concerns (ASA; Marketplace; ITEP).\n- **No direct action against ATUS:** There is no presidential-action-driven change to the ATUS ICR (the January 2026 renewal was approved \"without change\" as a routine nonsubstantive update), no negative policy note on the ATUS website, and no administration statement specifically announcing ATUS changes. The 2025 shutdown was a government-wide event, not targeted.\n- **Mitigants:** Enacted FY2026 appropriations were flat-to-slightly-up; Congress has a documented history of earmarking funds to maintain ATUS; the 2025 cycle was completed and published.\n\n**Assessment: Moderate Risk.** The changes affecting ATUS remain proposed and pending (reorganization, prioritization framework, FY27 cut request) rather than executed, and expert commentary anticipates rather than documents an administration decision against ATUS. This matches \"Proposed or pending changes; statements by administration officials suggesting a change is being considered or planned.\" There is no presidential-action-driven ICR change or negative policy note that would elevate this to High.\n\n---"
