      "evidence": "**Evidence \u2014 No Known Issue:**\n- ICR status: OMB control number 0607-0094 (\"Building Permits Survey Program\") is **Active**, approved May 1, 2024, expiring **May 31, 2027** (reginfo.gov, ICR 202402-0607-002).\n- Renewal is already in motion: a Federal Register notice published **October 1, 2026** requests an \"Extension, without Change, of a Currently Approved Collection\" for forms C-404 and C-411 (respondents: 19,987 and 3,200 respectively) \u2014 filed roughly eight months before expiration.\n- The 2025 annual BPS estimates were published (May 14, 2026), and the economic indicator release calendar extends normally into 2027 (December 2026 New Residential Construction scheduled for January 20, 2027) \u2014 evidence collection and publication are fully back on track.\n\n**Evidence \u2014 Moderate Risk:**\n- The **October 1 \u2013 November 12, 2025 government shutdown (43 days, the longest in history)** halted Census collection, processing, and publication. Monthly permit/housing-starts releases due in October\u2013November 2025 were delayed by roughly six weeks to two-plus months; the September and October reports were ultimately released together with a substantial lag (KPMG: \"The Census Bureau released both months of data with a lag due to the government shutdown\"; Reuters, Nov. 14, 2025, on delayed Census construction reports).\n- No month was permanently skipped and no data gap resulted (BPS respondents are local governments reporting from records they already hold, so collection could be recovered retroactively), but scheduled publication dates were plainly missed.\n\n**Assessment: Moderate Risk.** Collection and publication are continuing, the ICR is active with renewal already requested before expiration, and the annual release occurred. But the 2025 shutdown caused real, multi-month publication delays \u2014 squarely the rubric's \"typical or intended publication date missed / publication delayed\" \u2014 and the prospect of repeat funding lapses remains.\n\n---"
