      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- *No Known Issue level:* ICR 1220-0011 is **active**, approved without change March 25, 2025, expiring **August 31, 2027** (https://www.reginfo.gov/public/do/PRAViewICR?ref_nbr=202503-1220-001). Releases have been on schedule throughout 2026, including September 2026 data published October 2, 2026 (https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.htm), and schedules are posted months ahead (https://www.bls.gov/ces/publications/news-release-schedule.htm).\n- *Moderate Risk level:* The January 2026 Employment Situation was delayed from February 6 to February 11, 2026 due to a second, brief lapse in appropriations (https://www.bls.gov/bls/2025-lapse-revised-release-dates.htm; https://www.axios.com/2026/02/02/jobs-report-postponed-shutdown).\n- *High Risk level:* During the October 1\u2013November 13, 2025 shutdown, the **September 2025 Employment Situation was delayed ~7 weeks** (Oct 3 \u2192 Nov 20) and the **October 2025 Employment Situation release was canceled outright** \u2014 no monthly publication occurred in October 2025. CES establishment data for October were eventually published with the November release (collection period extended), but the statutory monthly publication cadence under 29 U.S.C. \u00a7 2 (\"at least once each month\") was missed (https://www.bls.gov/bls/2025-lapse-revised-release-dates.htm; https://www.bls.gov/cps/methods/2025-federal-government-shutdown-impact-cps.htm). State and metro CES releases were similarly delayed and canceled for October 2025.\n\n**Selected level: High Risk.** The rubric's High Risk triggers \u2014 \"statutory publication deadline missed and/or collection or publication skipped\" \u2014 literally occurred in October\u2013November 2025, and delays recurred in early 2026. Mitigating factors (data ultimately published, releases currently on schedule, ICR active through 2027) prevent any assessment of \"Gone,\" but two funding lapses within five months demonstrate that monthly continuity is hostage to appropriations.\n\n---"
