      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- *No Known Issue level:* National CES retains full monthly frequency, NAICS industry detail, hours and earnings series, and its ~119,000-business/622,000-worksite sample (https://www.bls.gov/ces/). The FY2026 budget explicitly prioritizes PFEIs, which shields CES relative to other BLS programs (https://www.dol.gov/sites/dolgov/files/general/budget/2026/CBJ-2026-V3-01.pdf).\n- *Moderate Risk level:* Record-low survey response rates (63.9% reported for March 2026) and eroding sample coverage increase reliance on imputation and the birth\u2013death model (https://democrats-edworkforce.house.gov/imo/media/doc/scott_letter_to_chairman_tim_walberg_re_hearing_on_federal_statistical_agencies.pdf).\n- *High Risk level:* (a) **Granularity reduced**: ~900 detailed state/area series eliminated with January 2026 data (see above). (b) **Timeliness reduced**: multi-week publication delays and a skipped monthly release in late 2025 (see above). (c) Benchmark revisions have been the largest since 2009 for two consecutive cycles (March 2024: \u2212818,000; March 2025: \u2212911,000 preliminary, \u2212862,000/\u2212898,000 final, including a banking-series data reconstruction), and the July 2026 report again included unusually large downward revisions (https://www.bls.gov/news.release/prebmk.nr0.htm; https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cesbmk.htm; Reuters, Aug 7, 2026). BLS staff and budget constraints \"led BLS to cancel products, samples, and services in 2025\" (https://www.amstat.org/docs/default-source/amstat-documents/the-nations-data-at-risk-2025/bureau-of-labor-statistics.pdf).\n\n**Selected level: High Risk.** Reductions in granularity and timeliness have already occurred (not merely threatened), and revision magnitude \u2014 a core quality metric \u2014 has deteriorated sharply, driven by declining sample coverage and response rates. Frequency of the national series is so far maintained.\n\n---"
