      "evidence": "**Evidence for \"High Risk\":**\n- **October 2025 collection was permanently skipped.** Due to the federal funding lapse (Oct 1\u2013Nov 12, 2025), CPS/HVS did not collect October data and it was not collected retroactively (BLS CPS FAQ: \"There are no CPS estimates for October 2025\"). Q4 2025 estimates are based on only November\u2013December data; Annual 2025 estimates exclude October entirely, with wider margins of error (Census Source & Accuracy statements). A strict reading of \"collection or publication skipped\" is satisfied by the permanently missing month.\n\n**Evidence for \"Moderate Risk\":**\n- **Q3 2025 publication was delayed, not skipped:** released December 11, 2025 instead of late October (~6 weeks late) per the Census economic indicators calendar.\n- **ICR lapsed only briefly:** OMB control no. 0607-0179 (previous clearance) expired 6/30/2026; the renewal package (ICR 202606-0607-001, \"Extension without change of a currently approved collection\") was received by OIRA 6/9/2026 and approved with change 8/21/2026, new expiration 8/31/2029 \u2014 an expiration gap of roughly seven weeks, well under one year.\n- **All scheduled products were still published:** Q4 2025 came out February 3, 2026 (near-normal timing), the Annual 2025 release followed, and Q1 2026 was released on schedule April 28, 2026. The 2026 release calendar is published (Q3 2026 due October 28, 2026), consistent with OMB Statistical Policy Directive 3 obligations for principal economic indicators.\n- The disruption was an external appropriations lapse, not a programmatic decision to curtail the survey.\n\n**Selected level: Moderate Risk.** Every quarterly and annual publication cycle was produced, the ICR is now active through 2029, and releases have returned to schedule. However, I flag this as borderline: the permanently skipped October 2025 collection month technically satisfies the High criterion under a literal reading, and the episode demonstrated that another appropriations lapse would immediately produce another unrecoverable gap. If your framework treats any permanently missing collection period as High, this category should be elevated accordingly.\n\n---"
