      "evidence": "**Evidence for No Known Issue:**\n- The ICR (OMB No. 0607-1029) is active and has been renewed repeatedly before expiration \u2014 most recent non-substantive change approved September 1, 2026; current clearance runs through 2028\u20132029 (reginfo.gov; program page).\n- Collections continue on the bi-monthly schedule: July 2026 data (collected July 15\u2013Aug 3) was released September 10, 2026; September and November 2026 collections were OMB-cleared (Federal Register notice of June 4, 2026, 2026-11172).\n- The March 2026 redesign actually *expanded* the sample (~136,000 housing units per wave, up from ~30,000 panel members) and added subnational estimates.\n\n**Evidence for Moderate Risk:**\n- The April 2025 release was delayed \u2014 the American Statistical Association's *The Nation's Data at Risk: 2025* report explicitly lists \"Census Bureau: Household Pulse Survey for April not yet released\" under Product Delays, amid the 2025 turbulence.\n- The October\u2013December 2025 government shutdown disrupted Census release schedules agency-wide (ASA report; multiple products delayed).\n- The March and May 2026 PUFs required re-release with corrected weights on September 10, 2026 \u2014 a roughly three-to-five-month lag in publishing *correct* data (User Note, 2603_HTOPS...09102026.pdf).\n- The program skipped/restructured collections during the 2024\u20132025 design transitions (cross-sectional \u2192 longitudinal \u2192 back to cross-sectional), leaving gaps and discontinuities in the series.\n\n**Evidence for High Risk:** One could argue a skipped collection (HPS-content releases appeared only in February, April, and June 2025 on the HPS pages, with the next HPS-content release not until March 2026), but the underlying HTOPS program continued collecting in the intervening months under non-HPS topical modules, so this is a publication-scope artifact of the design rather than a terminated collection.\n\n**Verdict: Moderate Risk.** The ICR is current and collections are proceeding, but the record since January 2025 shows real publication delays (April 2025, shutdown effects) and materially corrected re-releases (March/May 2026) \u2014 matching \"typical or intended publication date missed and/or publication delayed.\"\n\n---"
