      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- *Gone:* Not applicable \u2014 collection and publication continue.\n- *High Risk:* No reductions in granularity, timeliness, or frequency have occurred. The release remains monthly with full market-group, industry-group, and stage-of-process detail, supplements, and reliability tables. The 2025 annual revision (Nov. 24, 2025) incorporated the 2022 Economic Census, converted industry detail to 2022 NAICS, and updated market-group weights from BEA benchmark I-O accounts \u2014 i.e., quality was *improved* ([Dec. 3, 2025 release notice](https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/G17/20251203/g17.txt)).\n- *Moderate Risk:* The December 2025 release carried an explicit source-data caveat: some source data normally available were unavailable due to the shutdown, QPC preliminary Q3 data were never incorporated on the normal schedule (final data were used instead), and users were warned that \"care should be used when evaluating the reliability estimates\" \u2014 a documented, if temporary, quality anomaly. Looking forward, the Board's announced multi-year 10% headcount reduction and its effort to \"streamline our data offerings\" (the stated rationale for the DDP retirement) create an emerging risk to the capacity of the small Industrial Output Section to maintain current granularity and timeliness ([DDP announcement](https://www.federalreserve.gov/data/data-download-fred-information.htm); [Reuters, May 16, 2025](https://www.reuters.com/business/world-at-work/us-federal-reserve-aims-trim-staff-by-10-coming-years-2025-05-16/)).\n\n**Assessment: Moderate Risk.** No actual degradation has occurred, and the 2025 annual revision improved the series. But the rubric's \"potential or emerging risk\" standard is met: the demonstrated 2025 source-data vulnerability, the 10% multi-year staffing reduction plan, and the data-streamlining initiative at the producing agency together pose a credible emerging risk to granularity and timeliness.\n\n---"
