      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- *No Known Issue:* The dataset rests on explicitly named statutory authority: Board data collection/publication authority (12 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 244, 248); the QPC is conducted under 13 U.S.C. \u00a7 8(b), 50 U.S.C. \u00a7 98 et seq., and 12 U.S.C. \u00a7 244, and is co-sponsored by the Defense Logistics Agency for industrial-base mobilization planning ([Census QPC authority](https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/qpc/about.html); [89 FR 21235](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2024-03-27/html/2024-06479.htm)). Publication standards are binding under OMB Statistical Policy Directive No. 3 (as updated 2019/2023/2024), which specifies timeliness, scheduling, and revision discipline. The G.17 is a designated Principal Federal Economic Indicator, is required for implementation of federal programs (FOMC monetary policy under the Federal Reserve Act's dual mandate; defense industrial-base assessments under 50 U.S.C.), and has no substitute collection: it is the only official measure of U.S. industrial production and capacity (private alternatives like ISM indices or S&P Global PMIs are not equivalents).\n- *High Risk (counter-argument):* One could argue the monthly G.17 release itself is not mandated by a statute that says \"the Board shall publish industrial production monthly\" \u2014 it rests on Board practice plus the OMB directive framework, which technically governs by executive-branch statistical policy authority and long-standing Fed compliance rather than an express statutory publication mandate.\n\n**Assessment: No Known Issue.** The statutory and directive scaffolding is unusually strong and explicit for a statistical product: named statutory authority for the source survey, PFEI status under SPD-3, indispensable programmatic uses in monetary policy and defense mobilization, and no alternative data collection that could substitute.\n\n---"
