      "level": "High Risk",
      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- **Staffing:** AHRQ had ~300 staff; roughly half resigned after DOGE warnings, then 111 were fired on April 1, 2025 ([KFF Health News](https://kffhealthnews.org/health-industry/patient-safety-health-agency-dissolved-doge-federal-workforce-cuts-ahrq/)). AcademyHealth estimates depletion of **at least 65% since January 2025**; a Hill op-ed cites 80\u201390% ([Politico Pro](https://subscriber.politicopro.com/article/2025/11/work-on-critical-illnesses-halted-after-health-department-research-arm-whittles-staff-00633941); [The Hill](https://thehill.com/opinion/healthcare/5495565-agency-healthcare-quality-cuts/)). The FY27 budget appendix still lists 265 FTEs \u2014 \"independent estimates place current staffing at a fraction of that figure\" ([AcademyHealth](https://ahsituationreport.substack.com/p/what-the-fy27-presidents-budget-request)).\n- **Leadership:** AHRQ's leadership was purged and the agency administratively merged into the new HHS Office of Strategy as part of the March 27, 2025 DOGE-driven restructuring ([HHS press release](https://www.hhs.gov/press-room/hhs-restructuring-doge.html)); a new director was installed in 2025. The federal USAJobs listings tracker I checked (which archives Dec 31, 2025) corroborates the government-wide hiring collapse, though its JS-rendered table prevented extracting AHRQ-specific listing counts.\n- **Funding:** FY26 President's budget proposed a $129M (~35%) cut and elimination of AHRQ as an account; the House FY26 LHHS bill proposed **zeroing out AHRQ entirely** ([COSSA analysis](https://cossa.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/FY26-House-LHHS-Appropriations-Analysis.pdf)). **Congress rejected the reorganization and funded AHRQ at $345M for FY26** ([AcademyHealth](https://ahsituationreport.substack.com/p/what-the-fy27-presidents-budget-request)). The FY27 request repeats the cuts ($106\u2013129M depending on the document, with the MEPS-funding line halved). GAO is investigating whether ~$80M in unspent funds constitutes unlawful impoundment; grants staff were separated July 14, 2025, leaving the agency \"unable to process grant awards\" ([Boston Globe](https://www.bostonglobe.com/2025/08/10/business/trump-administration-research-waste/)).\n\n**Assessment: High Risk.** Multiple rubric thresholds are exceeded many times over: far more than 40% of staff lost, leadership removed, and budget cuts of 20%+ proposed (with the MEPS account specifically targeted for >50%). It is not \"Gone\" because Congress's FY26 appropriation ($345M) sustains operations, contractors (Westat/RTI/Census) carry the fieldwork, and MEPS was singled out for protection within the cuts.\n\n---"
