      "evidence": "**Evidence:**\n- The March 2025 restructuring \u2014 executed under President Trump's DOGE Executive Order \u2014 dissolved AHRQ and moved its functions into the Office of Strategy, whose mission is reoriented toward \"informing the Secretary's policies\" ([HHS](https://www.hhs.gov/press-room/hhs-restructuring-doge.html); [AcademyHealth](https://ahsituationreport.substack.com/p/what-the-fy27-presidents-budget-request)).\n- AcademyHealth (which opposes the merger) warns that combining deep cuts with a mission narrowed to the Secretary's priorities \"would fundamentally alter what these data programs are able to produce and for whom.\"\n- The September 2025 letter from the new AHRQ director describes the agency's surveys without naming MEPS, and announces plans to \"integrate and link these data with other real world data sources\" \u2014 a signal of a possible redesign ([SHADAC](https://shadac.umn.edu/news/federal-health-survey-data-releases-landscape-2025)).\n- Counterweights: the FY26/FY27 budgets explicitly *prioritize* AHRQ's statistical work (MEPS named) while eliminating grants; the IC ICR was renewed \"without change\"; the HC questionnaire revision now pending is a routine content update (adding social determinants, etc.) rather than content removal; no negative policy notes appear on the MEPS site; and Congress rejected the reorganization for FY26.\n- The 2025 MEPS website outage (see Category 1) occurred amid administration-driven web consolidations, contributing to a chilling effect even though data were restored.\n\n**Assessment: Moderate Risk.** The collection's content and publication cadence have not changed since January 2025, and the administration has repeatedly designated MEPS as protected \"statistical work.\" But there are concrete *pending* proposals (FY27 relocation of MEPS into the Office of Strategy, >50% cut to its funding line) and explicit official statements about integrating/linking the surveys with other data sources \u2014 squarely \"proposed or pending changes; statements by administration officials suggesting a change is being considered or planned.\" This borders on High Risk if one counts the already-executed, presidential-action-driven dissolution of the survey's home agency as a \"significant change in accordance with Administration priorities\"; I weigh it Moderate only because the change has not yet altered what MEPS collects or publishes, and Congress has pushed back once already.\n\n---"
